---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyFigure from '../components/CaseStudyFigure.astro';

const img = (file: string) => `images/case-studies/des-sys/${file}`;

const nav = [
  { label: 'Overview', href: '#overview' },
  { label: 'Problem: Tokens', href: '#tokens' },
  { label: 'Solution: AI Audit', href: '#audit' },
  { label: 'Challenge: Visual', href: '#visual' },
  { label: 'How Might We', href: '#hmw' },
  { label: 'Finding', href: '#finding' },
  { label: 'Solution: Visibility', href: '#specs' },
  { label: 'Takeaway', href: '#takeaway' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Design Systems"
  heading="Enterprise and 0 to 1 design systems"
  role="Product Designer"
  team="Intuit: sole designer, 1 developer, guidance from 2 designers. Portfolio: solo"
  timeline="Jan – Mar 2026, Oct 2026"
  projectType="Product, Design Systems"
  nav={nav}
  commentId="case-study-des-sys"
>
  <CaseStudyFigure
    slot="hero"
    src="videos/des-sys/colour-mode-switch.mp4"
    alt="The portfolio switching between light and dark mode: the new mode rises from the bottom of the screen behind a soft blurred edge"
    loop
    wide
  />
  <section id="overview" data-comment-id="des-sys-overview">
    <h2 class="section-label">Overview</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">
     Being on the Intuit Design System team, I learned that design system consistency is challenging to maintain between design and code. I took my learnings from working on a mature design system to building one from scratch for my portfolio.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tokens" data-comment-id="des-sys-intuit">
    <h2 class="section-label">Problem</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">Design and code don’t always use the right tokens. Hardcoded values and incorrect tokens kept theme updates from
          reaching parts of the UI.</p>
    <div class="decision-row des-sys-row">
      
      <CaseStudyFigure
        src={img('token-alignment.webp')}
        alt="Before and after: the checkbox label uses one font weight token in code and a semibold token in design, then both use the same token"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="audit" data-comment-id="des-sys-workflow">
    <h2 class="section-label">Solution</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">AI skills ran the audit to check for hardcoded values and token misalignment, then I previewed changes in code and in design.</p>
    <CaseStudyFigure
      src={img('audit-workflow-after.png')}
      alt="Designer-owned audit workflow with AI-assisted review, Storybook preview, and PR"
      wide
    />

    <h3 class="des-sys-subhead">Impact</h3>
    <p>I updated 3 core components. For the checkbox alone:</p>
    <div class="case-study-stats">
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>355k+</strong>
        <span>instances of checkbox in Figma</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>143</strong>
        <span>business units use checkbox in code</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>164</strong>
        <span>design teams use checkbox</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- <h3 class="des-sys-subhead">How the workflow changed</h3>
    <p>By the end of my term, Figma MCP let AI make the design changes too.</p> -->
  </section>

  <section id="visual" data-comment-id="des-sys-visual">
    <h2 class="section-label">Challenge</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">But 1 to 1 token alignment can still look misaligned. Text and layout render differently between design and code, so matching them takes deliberate adjustments that intentionally break 1 to 1 token alignment.</p>
    <div class="decision-row des-sys-row">
     
      <CaseStudyFigure
        src={img('visual-alignment.webp')}
        alt="Illustration: a checkbox label uses the same token in code and design but sits higher in code, then a hardcoded padding value lines it up"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="hmw" data-comment-id="des-sys-tradeoff">
    <h2 class="section-label">How might we</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">Would one source of truth bring everything into alignment?</p>
    <p>Large teams can’t leave Figma quickly, so I tested it on my portfolio, built code first.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="finding" data-comment-id="des-sys-portfolio">
    <h2 class="section-label">Finding</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">It fixed alignment, but not consistency.</p>
    <p>
      With no Figma to match, design and code can’t disagree. But AI still left near-duplicate text
      styles and mixed units.
    </p>
    <p>To be transparent, I vibe coded the first layouts, then wrote the system down with AI.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="specs" data-comment-id="des-sys-system">
    <h2 class="section-label">Solution</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">So I made the system visible, to proofread what AI generates.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <h3>DESIGN.md</h3>
        <p>
          The rules agents read before any UI work. The tokens themselves live in the CSS.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Automated checks</h3>
        <p>
          Flag any value that isn’t a token, and fail the build on drift.
        </p>
        <CaseStudyFigure
          src={img('docs-and-checks.webp')}
          alt="A DESIGN.md excerpt defining the spacing scale and its rule, next to the design check output reporting 0 off-token values"
        />
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Design system page</h3>
        <p>
          Every token and component state in one place, to review naming.
        </p>
        <CaseStudyFigure
          src={img('colour-roles.webp')}
          alt="Colour tokens on the design system page, named by role, shown half in light mode and half in dark mode"
          caption="Colours named by role, each with a light and a dark value."
        />
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Inspect mode</h3>
        <p>
          Hover a component to see its tokens and flag anything that isn’t one.
        </p>
        <CaseStudyFigure
          src={img('inspect-mode.webp')}
          alt="Inspect mode on the design system page, listing the tokens behind a secondary button: fill, text, type, border, padding, gap, and radius"
        />
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="case-study-stats">
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>10 → 7</strong>
        <span>text styles, after consolidating near-duplicates</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>4 → 3</strong>
        <span>line colours, each with one job</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-study-stat">
        <strong>323 → 0</strong>
        <span>off-token values after writing the system down</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="des-sys-after-figure">
      The checks missed the near-duplicates. I found them reading the
      <a class="text-link" href="/design-system">design system page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaway" data-comment-id="des-sys-takeaways">
    <h2 class="section-label">Takeaway</h2>
    <p class="heading text-ink">On both sides, AI speeds up the work, and people still have to check it.</p>
    <p>What carries over is making the system visible enough to proofread.</p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  /* Sub-sections within a section; specific enough to beat the layout's h3 reset */
  :global(.case-study-content) section > h3.des-sys-subhead {
    margin: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  /* A statement's text and figure, sitting below the statement */
  :global(.case-study-content) section > .des-sys-row {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  :global(.case-study-content) section > p.des-sys-after-figure {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  :global(.case-study-content) #specs .case-study-stats {
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
  }
</style>
